Operator is a physical and environmental object.
Setting is a physical and systemic process.
Pressing is a physical and systemic process.
Disengaging is a physical and systemic process.
Forcing is a physical and systemic process.
Catching is a physical and systemic process.
Trap is a physical and systemic object.
Trap can be set or sprung.
State sprung is initial.
Base is a physical and systemic object.
Animal is a physical and environmental object.
Animal can be free or caught.
State free is initial.
State caught is final.
Trigger is a physical and systemic object.
Trigger can be at rest or pressed.
State at rest is initial.
Locking Bar is a physical and systemic object.
Locking Bar can be engaged or disengaged.
Transverse Spring is a physical and systemic object.
Jaw is a physical and systemic object.
Jaw can be held back or released.
Trap consists of Trigger, Locking Bar, Transverse Spring, Jaw, and Base.
Operator handles Setting.
Setting changes Trap from sprung to set.
Animal handles Pressing.
Pressing requires Trigger.
Pressing changes Trigger from at rest to pressed.
Pressing invokes Disengaging.
Disengaging changes Locking Bar from engaged to disengaged.
Disengaging changes Trap from set to sprung.
Disengaging invokes Forcing.
Forcing requires Transverse Spring.
Forcing changes Jaw from held back to released.
Forcing invokes Catching.
Catching changes Animal from free to caught.
